  "synthesis_conclusions": [
    "Daylight is physically a source of electromagnetic radiation, unique in its spectral variability, and physiologically stimulates visual and circadian systems.",
    "Daylight and a view are highly desired psychologically.",
    "Daylight can maximize visual performance due to large amounts and excellent color rendering, but poor delivery can cause discomfort and reduce performance.",
    "People actively reduce daylight if it causes discomfort or task difficulty.",
    "Disrupted circadian rhythms negatively affect task performance and long-term health; bright daytime light (e.g., daylight) entrains the circadian system.",
    "Lighting conditions can change mood, but the effect of windows on mood is variable, depending on individual preferences, expectations, and habituation.",
    "The link between mood and productivity is weak and often overshadowed by other factors.",
    "Daylight has mixed health effects: outdoor exposure causes tissue damage but produces Vitamin D. Through glass, UV-B is reduced, but sensitive individuals can still be affected. Good daylighting reduces eyestrain, while poor daylighting increases it. Windows with a view reduce stress and healthcare demand. Daylight reduces health problems from electric light flicker.",
    "Windows increase construction/maintenance costs but can offset operating costs and positively affect rental value.",
    "Daylighting in retail spaces can positively affect sales."
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "Reducing the likelihood of discomfort from windows to minimize behaviors that limit daylight admission.",
    "Quantifying the financial return on windows in terms of what people are prepared to pay for them.",
    "Exploring the impact of daylight operating through the circadian system on task performance.",
    "Testing the biophilia hypothesis, particularly the relative importance of view out versus daylight."
  ],
